.\" gtk4sermo.1 — man page for gtk4sermo @PACKAGE_VERSION@
.\" haplo-dialog <devel@haplo-dialog.fr>
.\" This file is installed as /usr/share/man/man1/gtk4sermo.1
.\"
.TH GTK4SERMO 1 "October 2026" "sermo @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "sermo User Commands"
.SH NAME
gtk4sermo \- GTK4 dialog box creator for shell scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gtk4sermo
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.br
.B gtk4sermo
\fB\-\-program\fR=\fIVARIABLE\fR
.br
.B gtk4sermo
\fB\-\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
.br
.B gtk4sermo
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
.br
.IR VARIABLE =\(aq<window>...</window>\(aq
.B gtk4sermo
\fB\-\-program\fR=\fIVARIABLE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gtk4sermo
reads a GTK4 GUI description written in an XML dialect and displays
the resulting dialog window.
It is designed to be called from shell scripts to present interactive forms,
progress bars, list views, and other widgets without requiring knowledge of
GTK4 programming.
.PP
The GUI description can be supplied from an environment variable
.RB ( \-\-program ),
a file
.RB ( \-\-file ),
or standard input
.RB ( \-\-stdin ).
.PP
When an
.B exit:
action ends the dialog,
.B gtk4sermo
writes the current value of each named widget to standard output in the form:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
WIDGET_NAME=\(dqcurrent_value\(dq
.fi
.RE
.PP
Nothing else prints them. A
.B closewindow:
action that closes the last window prints only
\fBEXIT=\(dqclosewindow\(dq\fR, and closing the window from the window
manager prints only \fBEXIT=\(dqabort\(dq\fR.
.PP
Those lines escape the four characters the shell expands inside double quotes
.RB ( \e ,
.BR \(dq ,
.B $
and the backtick), so the calling shell may
.B eval
them. Bear in mind that a field's value is typed by whoever
.I uses
the dialog, who is not necessarily whoever wrote the script: if that matters,
prefer
.B \-\-do
instead, which passes the values through the environment and never reads them
back as code.
.SS Self\-executable dialog file
A dialog description read from a file may carry a shebang line, so the file
itself becomes executable:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
#!/usr/bin/gtk4sermo \-f
<window title="Hello">
  <vbox><text><label>Hi</label></text><button ok></button></vbox>
</window>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RB "Then " "chmod +x" " the file and run it directly."
Only the
.B first
line is dropped, and only when the description is read from a file
.RB ( \-f " / " \-\-file ).
It is not a comment syntax: a second line starting with
.B #
is a syntax error, and with
.B \-\-stdin
the line is not dropped at all.
.PP
The interpreter path must be one a package actually installs \(em
.BR /usr/bin/gtk4sermo .
The name
.B gtkdialog
exists only if the separate
.B sermo\-gtkdialog
package is installed as well (it points to gtk3sermo, not to gtk4sermo), so do not rely on it in a shebang.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-program= VARIABLE
Read the XML dialog description from the environment variable
.IR VARIABLE .
.TP
.BI \-\-file= FILE
Read the XML dialog description from
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B \-\-stdin
Read the XML dialog description from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-\-do= CMD
After the dialog has closed, run the shell command
.IR CMD .
The widget values are exported as environment variables first, so
.I CMD
can read them directly. The command goes through the same path as every other
command the program runs: a direct
.BR exec ()
without a shell when
.I CMD
carries no shell metacharacter, a logged fallback to the shell
.RB ( SERMO_SHELL ,
or
.B /bin/sh
by default) when it does, and a clean refusal when
.B SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK=1
is set. The exit status of the dialog is not replaced by that of
.IR CMD ;
a non-zero status is reported as a warning.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Echo every character read from the dialog description as it is fed to the lexer.
Useful for diagnosing parser issues.
.TP
.BI \-i " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-include=" FILE
Make the shell functions and variables of
.I FILE
available to every action. The file is not run once at start\-up: each shell
command launched by an action is turned into
.RB \(dq ". \fIFILE\fP; \fIcommand\fP" \(dq,
so the file is sourced afresh in every action's sub\-shell. Nothing is shared
between two actions except what
.I FILE
itself defines.
.IP
A relative
.I FILE
is resolved against the directory the program was started from: under
.BR /bin/sh ,
.B .
would otherwise look it up in
.BR PATH .
.IP
.B \-\-do
is the exception: the command it runs is
.B not
prefixed, so it does not see the included file.
.TP
.BI \-e " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-event\-driven=" FILE
Event-driven program. The shell first reads
.I FILE
.RB ( ". \fIFILE\fP" ),
then gtk4sermo runs again with
.BI \-\-include= FILE\fR,
keeping
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-c ,
.B \-\-print-ir
and
.BR \-\-program .
So
.I FILE
can set the variable that holds the dialog and define the shell functions its
actions call.
.TP
.BI \-g " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-glade\-xml=" FILE
Build the dialog from
.IR FILE ,
a GtkBuilder interface description (the format Glade saves), instead of the
XML dialog language. The window shown is the object whose id is given by
.B \-\-program
.RB ( MAIN_WINDOW
by default); every widget with an id becomes a variable of that name. A signal
handler is not a C function but a sermo action \- a shell command,
.BR exit: ,
.BR refresh: ...
\- run under the same rules as an
.B <action>
.RB ( SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS ,
.BR SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK ,
.BR \-\-include ).
A handler on
.B realize
is the widget's input: its output fills the widget. The file must be written for
GTK 4: GTK 4 does not read GTK 3 interface files. An unreadable file, or no window with that id, ends the program
with status 1.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-center
Place the window at the centre of the screen.
.TP
.BI \-G " GEOMETRY" "\fR,\fP \-\-geometry=" GEOMETRY
Placement and size of the window,
.RI [ W x H ][ + X + Y ]
following the X11 convention (e.g. \fB800x600+0+0\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-print\-ir
Parse the description, print the intermediate representation, and exit without
opening any window. Needs no X or Wayland display: this is what the regression
suite uses.
.TP
.BI \-\-render\-png= FILE
Draw the dialog off screen, write it to the PNG image
.IR FILE ,
and exit without entering the event loop. A display is still needed; a virtual
one
.RB ( "xvfb\-run \-a" )
will do.
.TP
.BI \-\-space\-expand= BOOL
Default value of the
.B space-expand
attribute for every widget.
.TP
.BI \-\-space\-fill= BOOL
Default value of the
.B space-fill
attribute for every widget.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-no\-warning
Suppress warning messages.
.SH WIDGETS
gtk4sermo reads the same tags as the other ports, listed in
.BR sermo (1),
among them
.BR grid ", " paned ", " stack ", " flowbox ", " overlay ", " revealer ,
.BR wizard ", " toolbar " and " menubutton .
The XML language is described in
.BR gtk4sermo\-xml (5).
.PP
Each named widget gives its value as a line
\fINAME\fR=\(dq\fIvalue\fR\(dq
on standard output when an \fBexit:\fR action ends the dialog. Nothing is exported to the calling
shell: read the lines with
.B eval
or, safer, use
.BR \-\-do ,
whose command gets the values in its environment.
.SH SOURCES AND TARGETS
.nh
Some tags read a device or a service instead of running a command. The text of
the tag is an all\-in\-one address:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
<input modbus>tcp://plc:502?esclave=1&registre=1</input>
<input opcua>opc.tcp://server:4840?noeud=ns=1;s=pieces</input>
<input mqtt>mqtt://broker:1883?sujet=maison/salon/temperature</input>
<input mavlink>udp://:14550?champ=batterie</input>
<input media>media://lounge?donnee=etat</input>
<action mqtt>mqtt://broker?sujet=maison/salon/lampe&valeur=on</action>
<action mavlink>tcp://127.0.0.1:5760?ordre=rtl</action>
<action media>media://lounge?commande=pause</action>
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
An
.RB < input >
of this kind only reads: nothing is written to the device.
.IP \(bu 2
.RB < "action mqtt" >,
.RB < "action mavlink" >
and
.RB < "action media" >
are the only writes. They are allowed only in a
.RB < button >
or a
.RB < menuitem >
(a write needs a click); anywhere else the dialog is refused when it is read.
A command that names a
.B sermo\-cible\-
program by hand is refused too.
.IP \(bu 2
The tag becomes a command that runs a helper program,
.BI /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-source\- PROTOCOL
(reading) or
.BI /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-cible\- PROTOCOL
(writing). The address is passed as one argument, percent\-encoded, never
through the shell.
.B SERMO_SOURCE_DIR
changes the folder; it must be an absolute path. With
.BR SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS ,
name the helper (for example
.BR /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-source\-modbus ).
.IP \(bu 2
Each helper comes in its own package:
.BR sermo\-source\-modbus ,
.BR sermo\-source\-opcua ,
.BR sermo\-source\-mqtt ,
.BR sermo\-source\-mavlink ,
.BR sermo\-source\-media ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-mqtt ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-mavlink ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-media .
.IP \(bu 2
MQTT and MAVLink writes go only to what an allow\-list names:
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mqtt
or
.IR /etc/sermo/cibles\-mqtt ,
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mavlink
or
.IR /etc/sermo/cibles\-mavlink .
Without a list, nothing is sent.
.IP \(bu 2
MQTT: credentials
.RB ( identifiants= )
on
.B mqtt://
(not encrypted) are refused before any connection, unless the address carries
.BR clair=oui .
.B mqtts://
requires
.B confiance=
(the authority that signed the server certificate).
.IP \(bu 2
MAVLink: telemetry; a click sets one parameter while the drone is disarmed,
gives a safety order
.RB ( rtl ", " land ,
or
.B terminaison
to cut the motors, which needs a second press within 5 s), or sends a mission
that is never started. No piloting: no arming, takeoff, mode change or
movement.
.IP \(bu 2
Media: sound and video played by mpv. A network source plays only inside
.BR bwrap ;
a local file plays inside
.B bwrap
when it is installed, and otherwise without a sandbox (a message says so).
.PP
Details (every parameter, every refusal): the user manual,
.B MANUEL_UTILISATEUR.en.md
section 7.3, in the sources at
.IR https://gitlab.com/haplo\-dialog/sermo .
.hy
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Widget values are exported as environment variables before executing
.B \-\-do
commands.
.TP
.B GTK_THEME
Override the GTK theme, e.g.
.BR GTK_THEME=Adwaita:dark .
.TP
.B SERMO_DARK
Force the dark or the light Adwaita variant: a value that starts with
.B 0
gives
.BR GTK_THEME=Adwaita ,
any other non\-empty value
.BR GTK_THEME=Adwaita:dark .
It has no effect when
.B GTK_THEME
is already set.
.TP
.B SERMO_INPUT_MAX
The most bytes one
.RB < input >
reads, from a command or a file: 16 MiB (16777216) by default,
.B 0
for no limit. Beyond it, reading stops as if at end of file, and one warning is
printed on standard error, never on standard output. A value that is not a
number of bytes is reported, and the default applies. The progress bar is not
capped: it keeps one line at a time.
.TP
.B SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK
When set (to any value), refuse the shell fallback (see
.BR SERMO_SHELL )
used for
.RB < input >/< action >
commands that contain shell metacharacters, and fail closed instead. Commands
without shell metacharacters still run via direct
.BR exec ()
(no shell). Use this to harden gtk4sermo when rendering XML whose command
strings may come from a less-trusted source than the local script author (see
.B safe_exec.c
design note 1).
.TP
.B SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS
A comma-separated list of command names that the program is allowed to run, for
example
.BR SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS=ls,cat,date .
Unset by default, and deliberately so: the XML language exists to run commands,
and the shipped examples invoke around twenty of them — fourteen call
.B bash
or
.B sh
directly. A list that were on by default would break the product without
protecting anyone, since the command comes from the very script the caller
wrote. It is meant for the other case: someone DEPLOYING a dialog into a
less-trusted context (a kiosk, a guest session) who wants to bound what it can
launch.
.IP
The comparison is on the command as it will be run, never on its base name
alone: an entry without a slash allows that name and the path
.B PATH
resolves it to, an entry with a slash allows exactly that path, and a program
that bears an allowed name but lives elsewhere is refused. While the list is
set, the shell fallback is refused as well \(em otherwise
.B sh \-c \(aq...\(aq
would walk straight through the list by calling itself
.BR sh .
The tags of SOURCES AND TARGETS run a helper program: name it in the list.
.TP
.B SERMO_SHELL
The shell used for the fallback: the absolute path of an executable program
(for example
.BR SERMO_SHELL=/bin/bash ).
Unset or empty: the default shell,
.B /bin/sh
(under Windows,
.B %ComSpec%
or else
.BR cmd.exe ).
A relative path, or a file that cannot be run, is reported once on standard
error and the default shell is used.
.TP
.B SERMO_SOURCE_DIR
The folder of the helper programs that
.RB < "input modbus" >,
.RB < "input mqtt" >,
.RB < "action mqtt" >...
run (see SOURCES AND TARGETS):
.B /usr/libexec/sermo
by default. It must be an absolute path made of safe characters (letters,
digits and
.BR ._:/=,+@\- );
otherwise the dialog is refused when it is read.
.TP
.B GTKDIALOG_PIXMAP_PATH
Colon\-separated list of directories in which gtk4sermo searches for image
files that are not found at the path given in the XML. It is consulted when
loading
.BR <pixmap> ,
window and button icons, and menu\-item images.
.TP
.B MAIN_DIALOG
Fallback source variable. When none of
.BR \-\-program ,
.BR \-\-file ,
.BR \-\-stdin ,
.B \-\-event\-driven
or
.B \-\-glade\-xml
is given, gtk4sermo reads the GUI description from the environment variable
named
.BR MAIN_DIALOG .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/sermo\-backend\-gtk4/
Copyright and Debian changelog of the package.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/sermo\-doc/examples/
Example scripts (package
.BR sermo\-doc ).
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/sermo/
The helper programs of SOURCES AND TARGETS
.RB ( sermo\-source\-* ", " sermo\-cible\-* ),
one package each.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mqtt\fR, \fI/etc/sermo/cibles\-mqtt
The allow\-list of
.RB < "action mqtt" >:
yours (mode 600) and root's (mode 644). Without a list, nothing is sent.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mavlink\fR, \fI/etc/sermo/cibles\-mavlink
The allow\-list of
.RB < "action mavlink" >,
same rules.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/gtk4sermo.1.gz
This manual page.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Simple yes/no dialog
.nf
export DIALOG='
<window title="Confirm">
  <vbox>
    <text><label>Proceed?</label></text>
    <hbox>
      <button ok></button>
      <button cancel></button>
    </hbox>
  </vbox>
</window>'
gtk4sermo --program=DIALOG
.fi
.SS Read entry value
.nf
export DIALOG='
<window>
  <vbox>
    <entry><variable>NAME</variable></entry>
    <button ok></button>
  </vbox>
</window>'
gtk4sermo --program=DIALOG --do='echo "Name: $NAME"'
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The full manual, with examples, is installed by the package:
.IP
.B info gtk4sermo
.PP
